# KNOWN GAP, SELF-HOST lane — a USER-DEFINED generic method that takes a
# callable cannot be compiled on the self-host lane at all, on ANY spelling of
# the argument.
#
# `U transform[U, F](self, F f)` needs `F` bound from the argument (shape 1) and
# `U = F.return_type` (shape 2). The self-host's mirror
# (`self_host_typechecker/traits.gg::infer_method_targs`) has both shapes, but
# the emitted C assigns the call's result into a `Str` slot from an `int`:
#
#     error: incompatible types when assigning to type 'Str' from type 'int'
#
# MEASURED identical BEFORE and AFTER R48 Track A's `t0770` readers, and on
# every spelling — top-level function, closure literal, and `Callable` PARAM —
# so it is a PRE-EXISTING self-host lag, not that round's inflow. Rust gg
# compiles and runs all three spellings correctly (see the live fixture
# `tests/fixtures/generic_method_callable_param/targ_inference.gg`), which is
# why that fixture is NOT top-level: a top-level placement would register this
# self-host non-MATCH against `RUNTIME_DIFF_NONMATCH_CEILING`.
#
# ⚠ DISCRIMINATE: the self-host's shape-2 loop has the SAME `RTFunction`-only
# predicate that made Rust gg's reader 6 blind to a `CallableTrait` wrapper —
# but fixing only that will NOT close this gap, because the TOP-LEVEL-FN
# spelling (a plain `RTFunction`, no wrapper anywhere) fails identically. The
# defect is downstream of the binding.
#
# NOT `t0877` (a closure LITERAL's own body typed I64 by `guess_return_type`) —
# this one has no closure literal in the failing control.
#
# INTENDED, on the self-host lane: prints `hello!` then `5` then `hello?`,
# rc 0 — matching Rust gg, which already does.

from std.conv import int_to_str

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct Holder:
    String name

equip Holder:
    U transform[U, F](self, F f): return f(self.name)

String bang(String s):
    return mk(s, "!")

int length_of(String s):
    return s.len()

String via_callable_param(Callable[String(String)] f):
    Holder h = Holder(mk("hel", "lo"))
    return h.transform(f)

void main():
    Holder h = Holder(mk("hel", "lo"))
    print(h.transform(bang))
    print(int_to_str(h.transform(length_of)))
    print(via_callable_param((String s): mk(s, "?")))
